language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ multi: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ This is a multi-commodity transportation problem. There are origins, destinations and products. Each origin has a certain supply of each product, and each destination has a certain demand for each product. The cost of shipping one unit of a product from an origin to a destination depends on the origin, the destination and the product. Each lane from an origin to a destination has a limit on the total amount shipped along it, over all products. Ship all the products from the origins to the destinations so as to minimise the total shipping cost.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `multi.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/multi.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `multi_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py multi.json
$   savilerow model.eprime multi_1.param -run-solver

$ number of origins and number of products, derived from the data: the rows and columns of supply (the reader's JSON has no such field)
given supply_dim1, supply_dim2 : int
$ number of destinations, derived from the data: the number of rows of demand (the reader's JSON has no such field)
given demand_dim1 : int
$ supply[i,p]: the supply of product p at origin i (indices 1-based: the data's index 0 is 1)
given supply : matrix indexed by [int(1..supply_dim1), int(1..supply_dim2)] of int
$ demand[j,p]: the demand for product p at destination j
given demand : matrix indexed by [int(1..demand_dim1), int(1..supply_dim2)] of int
$ limit[i,j]: the limit on the total amount shipped from origin i to destination j, over all products
given limit : matrix indexed by [int(1..supply_dim1), int(1..demand_dim1)] of int
$ cost[i,j,p]: the cost of shipping one unit of product p from origin i to destination j
given cost : matrix indexed by [int(1..supply_dim1), int(1..demand_dim1), int(1..supply_dim2)] of int

letting origins = supply_dim1
letting products = supply_dim2
letting destinations = demand_dim1

$ the total demand of all destinations for all products: an upper bound on any amount shipped
letting total_demand = sum j : int(1..destinations) . sum p : int(1..products) . demand[j,p]
$ an upper bound on the total cost: the sum of all unit costs times the total demand
letting total_unit_cost = sum i : int(1..origins) . sum j : int(1..destinations) . sum p : int(1..products) . cost[i,j,p]

$ x[i,j,p]: the amount of product p shipped from origin i to destination j
find x : matrix indexed by [int(1..origins), int(1..destinations), int(1..products)] of int(0..total_demand)
$ total_cost: the total cost of the shipments
find total_cost : int(0..total_unit_cost * total_demand)

minimising total_cost
such that
$ the amount of each product shipped out of an origin does not exceed its supply
forAll i : int(1..origins) . forAll p : int(1..products) . ((sum j : int(1..destinations) . x[i,j,p]) <= supply[i,p]),
$ the amount of each product delivered to a destination is exactly its demand
forAll j : int(1..destinations) . forAll p : int(1..products) . ((sum i : int(1..origins) . x[i,j,p]) = demand[j,p]),
$ the total amount shipped along each lane, over all products, does not exceed the lane's limit
forAll i : int(1..origins) . forAll j : int(1..destinations) . ((sum p : int(1..products) . x[i,j,p]) <= limit[i,j]),
$ the total cost is the sum of the costs of all shipments
total_cost = (sum i : int(1..origins) . sum j : int(1..destinations) . sum p : int(1..products) . cost[i,j,p] * x[i,j,p])
